winewayland: place a client subsurface on the edges of its window rect, rounded outward

Premiere Pro's Program Monitor showed a thin black L along the left and bottom of the video
view -- static, immune to repaints and to resizing the panel, sitting between the GL child's
own pillarbox grey and the panel's grey border. It is not drawn by anyone: it is a strip of
the toplevel surface that nothing paints, exposed because two paths derive the child's
on-screen rect from the same window rect and disagree by one pixel.

  (a) What the parent stops painting. WS_CLIPCHILDREN / DCX_CLIPCHILDREN are resolved in the
      server: clip_children() (server/window.c) subtracts each visible child's visible_rect,
      in raw device pixels, exactly. Everything the parent draws through that DC -- here the
      Direct2D DC-direct blit-back -- leaves the child's rect untouched.

  (b) What the child covers. wayland_surface_reconfigure_client() converted the ORIGIN and the
      EXTENT of the child's client rect to logical coordinates separately, each with
      round(v / scale), then wl_subsurface_set_position + wp_viewport_set_destination. At a
      fractional scale the logical grid is coarser than the device grid, so round-trip
      recovers the rect only when both the origin and the far edge happen to sit within half
      a device pixel of the logical grid.

Measured on the dev box (3840x2160 @ scale 1.7, neutron-wine 11.10-87, log
premiere-20260914-144822.log): video view hwnd 0x101d8, client == window == visible rect,
2128x854 device px, on toplevel 0x10122. The driver logged
    reconfigure_client subsurface=991,122+1252x502
i.e. round(2128/1.7)=1252, round(854/1.7)=502 -- the extent is right. Placing 991,122 back on
the device grid gives left 1685, top 207, right 3813, bottom 1061. The log prints only the
rounded output, which admits an origin of cx in {1684,1685}, cy in {207,208}; of those four,
only cx=1684, cy=208 yields a child that starts one pixel right of the exclusion (1685 vs
1684) and ends one pixel above it (1061 vs 1062), with the top and right edges overlapping
by one and invisible. That is the operator's L, uniquely, so the specific case is fixed by the
observation rather than read from the log.

Rounding each EDGE to nearest does not fix it: 1684/1.7 = 990.6 rounds to 991 and lands on
1685 again. A device coordinate that is not within half a pixel of a multiple of the scale
cannot be hit by an integer logical position at all, so the child cannot be made to cover
exactly the exclusion in general. What it can always do is cover no less: round the near
edges DOWN and the far edges UP. For the four candidate origins above this gives, per edge,
an overlap of 0-2 device px over painted parent content and never a gap. The overlap hides at
most two rows/columns of the parent right beside the child; the gap was a black line.

The buffer is unchanged; only the viewport destination grows by up to two logical px per
axis, which the compositor already resamples (1252*1.7 = 2128.4 was never 1:1 either).
wayland_client_surface_create()'s initial destination (the 2026-08-31 fix for the oversized
first present) still uses the nearest-rounded extent: it has no position to work from and is
replaced by reconfigure_client on attach a few ms later; the defect it fixed was an ABSENT
destination, and a 1-2 px difference for one frame is not that defect. The frame offset is
folded into the origin before conversion, as before, so both edges carry it once.

Scope: client subsurfaces only (the path that covers a parent exclusion). Owned-window
subsurfaces, toplevel sizing, the frame-spill crop and the maximized-configure path are
untouched. Kill switch NEUTRON_CLIENT_EDGE_ROUND=0 restores the previous arithmetic for A/B
without a rebuild; NEUTRON_WL_DIAG=1 reports both results at the outcome.

wayland_surface_reconfigure_client() is upstream Wine code and upstream behaves the same
way; Windows shows no seam because the child's swapchain covers exactly the rect the parent
excludes. Third upstream-worthy divergence found on this project, alongside the
NtGdiExtTextOutW alignment and the SB_CTL painted guard.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
@@ -1441,9 +1441,54 @@
     client_width = client_rect->right - client_rect->left;
     client_height = client_rect->bottom - client_rect->top;
 
-    wayland_surface_coords_from_window(surface, client_x, client_y, &x, &y);
-    wayland_surface_coords_from_window(surface, client_width, client_height,
-                                       &width, &height);
+    /* NEUTRON: convert the rect's EDGES, rounded outward, not its origin and extent.
+     *
+     * The parent stops painting exactly the child's rect: WS_CLIPCHILDREN / DCX_CLIPCHILDREN
+     * subtract the child's visible_rect in device pixels (server/window.c clip_children).
+     * Converting the origin and the extent separately with round(v / scale) placed the
+     * subsurface up to one device pixel inside that rect on any edge, and the strip in
+     * between is painted by nobody. Measured on Premiere's Program Monitor at scale 1.7:
+     * client 2128x854 at a 1684,208 origin became subsurface 991,122+1252x502, which sits
+     * at device 1685..3813 x 207..1061 -- a one-pixel black L along the left and bottom.
+     *
+     * A device coordinate that is not within half a pixel of a multiple of the scale cannot
+     * be hit by an integer logical position at all, so exact coverage is not available in
+     * general. Covering NO LESS always is: floor the near edges, ceil the far edges. The
+     * child then overlaps at most two device rows/columns of painted parent content beside
+     * it, and never leaves a gap. Kill switch NEUTRON_CLIENT_EDGE_ROUND=0 restores the old
+     * arithmetic for an A/B without a rebuild. */
+    {
+        static int edge_round = -1;
+        int old_x, old_y, old_w, old_h;
+
+        if (edge_round < 0) edge_round = neutron_gate("NEUTRON_CLIENT_EDGE_ROUND", TRUE);
+
+        wayland_surface_coords_from_window(surface, client_x, client_y, &old_x, &old_y);
+        wayland_surface_coords_from_window(surface, client_width, client_height,
+                                           &old_w, &old_h);
+        if (edge_round)
+        {
+            double scale = surface->window.scale;
+            int x2 = (int)ceil((client_x + client_width) / scale);
+            int y2 = (int)ceil((client_y + client_height) / scale);
+
+            x = (int)floor(client_x / scale);
+            y = (int)floor(client_y / scale);
+            width = x2 - x;
+            height = y2 - y;
+        }
+        else
+        {
+            x = old_x; y = old_y; width = old_w; height = old_h;
+        }
+
+        if (neutron_wl_surf_diag() && (x != old_x || y != old_y || width != old_w || height != old_h))
+            ERR("neutron-clientedge: hwnd=%p client=(%d,%d)-(%d,%d) win px scale=%.4f -> "
+                "subsurface %d,%d+%dx%d (origin/extent rounding gave %d,%d+%dx%d)\n",
+                surface->hwnd, client_x, client_y, client_x + client_width,
+                client_y + client_height, surface->window.scale,
+                x, y, width, height, old_x, old_y, old_w, old_h);
+    }
 
     TRACE("hwnd=%p subsurface=%d,%d+%dx%d\n", surface->hwnd, x, y, width, height);
 
